            <ArticleTitle>Coronary thrombosis related to use of Xenadrine RFA.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>74-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Recently, ephedra was removed from the U.S. marketplace due to a heightened concern that dietary supplements containing ephedra may present &quot;an unreasonable risk of illness or injury.&quot; This is the 1st time the U.S. Food and Drug Administration has banned an herbal supplement, and the ban sheds light on the potential harm of nutritional supplements that are used for weight loss or as a boost to athletic performance. We report the case of a body builder who used Xenadrine RFA, an ephedra-containing supplement, at recommended doses for nearly a year; he then experienced an acute myocardial infarction, which was documented to be secondary to thrombosis in situ. We ruled out other possible causes of myocardial infarction, as well a hypercoagulable state. There was no evidence of illicit drug use. Our report serves as a poignant reminder of the potential dangers of herbal supplementation, especially when used to heighten athletic performance.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
